\section{Analysis}
\label{sec:analysis}

We test the three predictions of the confirmation bias hypothesis (\S\ref{sec:mirage}).

\subsection{Condorcet Effective-K}
\label{sec:effective-k}

Table~\ref{tab:independence} reports Fleiss' $\kappa$ and Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}) across 15 conditions at $K = 12$. Among instruction-tuned models, $\kappa$ ranges from 0.21 to 0.82, yielding $K_{\text{eff}} = 1.20\text{--}3.62$. Across four Mistral-7B seeds, $\kappa \in [0.41, 0.51]$ yet $\Delta$ ranges from $+3.43$ to $-0.49$\,pp, indicating seed-level constraint quality matters beyond independence. Positive $\Delta$ requires both sufficient $K_{\text{eff}}$ ($\leq 1.30$ never improves) and SC accuracy ($K_{\text{eff}} = 3.62$ with SC $= 39\%$ degrades). All four model families produce $\kappa \geq 0.41$ (Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Trace independence metrics ($K = 12$, sorted by $\kappa$ descending). $\kappa$: Fleiss' Kappa. $K_{\text{eff}}$: Effective-K (Eq.~\ref{eq:effk}). SC: Self-Consistency baseline. $\Delta$: SICA $-$ SC (pp). {*}\,$p < 0.05$ (uncorrected; BH adj.\ $p = 0.112$, $m{=}16$); positive $\Delta$ values do not reach significance. multi-T $= T \in \{0.3, 0.7, 1.2\}$, 4 traces each.}
\label{tab:independence}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccrr@{}}
\toprule
Model & Condition & $\kappa$ & $K_{\text{eff}}$ & SC (\%) & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Qwen3 (think) & FOLIO & 0.91 & 1.09 & ${\sim}$84 & ${\sim}$0 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & LogiQA & 0.82 & 1.20 & 78.50 & $-$0.50 \\
Qwen3 & FOLIO & 0.77 & 1.26 & 84.80 & $-$0.49 \\
Qwen2.5-14B & FOLIO & 0.74 & 1.30 & 75.49 & $-$0.98 \\
Qwen2.5-7B & FOLIO & 0.66 & 1.46 & 75.98 & $+$0.49 \\
\addlinespace
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.3$) & FOLIO & 0.53 & 1.77 & 58.33 & $-$1.47 \\
Mistral-7B & LogiQA & 0.51 & 1.81 & 51.00 & $+$0.50 \\
Mistral-7B (s456) & FOLIO & 0.51 & 1.82 & 55.88 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & FOLIO & 0.50 & 1.84 & 55.88 & $+$3.43 \\
Mistral-7B (s123) & FOLIO & 0.49 & 1.88 & 54.41 & $+$2.94 \\
Mistral-7B (s789) & FOLIO & 0.41 & 2.17 & 54.41 & $-$0.49 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}0.5$) & FOLIO & 0.52 & 1.80 & 58.33 & $-$0.98 \\
Mistral-7B (multi-T) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 57.84 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B ($T{=}1.0$) & FOLIO & 0.48 & 1.91 & 55.39 & 0.00 \\
Mistral-7B & PW-D5 & 0.21 & 3.62 & 39.33 & $\mathbf{-2.83}${*} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}


\subsection{Symbolic Confirmation Bias}
\label{sec:confirmation-bias}

\looseness=-1 Self-extracted constraints systematically confirm the model's majority answer. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, when SICA errs, 83.3\% of constraints support the incorrect answer---bias ratio $4.97\times$ (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias})---and 94\% of errors overlap with SC. Z3 rejects only 3.4\% of constraints, confirming degeneration (Eq.~\ref{eq:degenerate}).

\looseness=-1 Bias generalizes: $4.97\times$ [3.98, 6.37] (Mistral, 12-trace), $3.93\times$ [2.44, 7.11] (Qwen-14B, 12-trace), $2.88\times$ (LLaMA, 4-trace$^{\dag}$), all $> 1.0$ (95\% bootstrap CIs for Mistral/Qwen; Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-bias}; weighted BRs in Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model-bias}). Constraints use trace-specific variables (56.4\% zero cross-answer overlap), collapsing aggregation to majority voting.

\looseness=-1 Two controls rule out answer leakage. First, BR is uniform across early ($5.37$), mid ($5.25$), and late ($5.16$) reasoning steps (Fisher $p = 0.71$); high-consensus errors (SC $\geq 10/12$) amplify to $\text{BR} = 18.8\times$ versus $2.7\times$ for low-consensus ($p < 0.001$). Second, removing the answer sentence from CoT traces before extraction yields no BR decrease across three truncation strategies (Wilcoxon $p \geq 0.12$, smallest $p = 0.12$, conservative truncation; per-problem mean BR: full $2.79$, truncated $2.26$--$2.66$, computed over problems with finite ratio) despite significant accuracy loss (McNemar $p < 0.013$). SCB is consistent with a distributional prior embedded throughout reasoning, not answer contamination.

\subsection{Process-Level Independence}
\label{sec:process-kappa}

We define process similarity as the mean pairwise cosine similarity of TF-IDF trace representations:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:process-sim}
\text{ProcessSim}(q) = \frac{2}{K(K{-}1)} \sum_{i < j} \cos(\mathbf{v}_i^q, \mathbf{v}_j^q),
\end{equation}
where $\mathbf{v}_i^q$ is the TF-IDF vector of trace $t_i$ for question $q$. On Mistral-7B FOLIO, mean ProcessSim $= 0.69$, far exceeding answer-level $\kappa = 0.46$: traces share reasoning processes even when answers diverge. The gap generalizes across four families (Table~\ref{tab:cross-model-process}), ranging from $0.31$ (LLaMA) to $0.02$ (Qwen-14B), revealing hidden process homogeneity that answer diversity masks.


\subsection{Cumulative Information Lift}
\label{sec:cil}

\looseness=-1 We unify the preceding diagnostics under \textit{Cumulative Information Lift} (CIL):
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:cil-main}
\text{CIL}(V, G) = \log_2 \frac{P(V \text{ correct} \mid G \text{ wrong})}{P(V \text{ correct} \mid G \text{ correct})}.
\end{equation}
$\text{CIL} > 0$ when verifier $V$ preferentially corrects generator $G$'s errors; $\text{CIL} < 0$ indicates shared bias. Table~\ref{tab:cil_main} reports CIL for eight verifier--generator pairs. All cross-architecture NLI pairs on ProofWriter yield $\text{CIL} > 0$, while same-model SICA yields $\text{CIL} < 0$. CIL strongly predicts combo gain (Spearman $\rho = 0.881$, $p = 0.004$; Pearson $r = 0.854$, $p = 0.008$), unifying three findings: (1)~SCB corresponds to $\text{CIL}(\text{self}, G) \ll 0$; (2)~cross-architecture escape succeeds when $\text{CIL} > 0$; (3)~CIL decays toward zero as generator capability rises, explaining the aggregation ceiling.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\caption{Cumulative Information Lift across eight verifier--generator conditions. $\text{CIL} > 0$: cross-arch NLI on ProofWriter. $\text{CIL} < 0$: same-model SICA or weak capability gap.}
\label{tab:cil_main}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llrr@{}}
\toprule
Generator--Verifier & Dataset & CIL & $\Delta$ (pp) \\
\midrule
Mistral $\times$ BART-NLI & PW & $+$1.86 & $+$5.17 \\
Mistral $\times$ DeBERTa-NLI & PW & $+$1.67 & $+$6.67 \\
Mistral $\times$ RoBERTa-NLI & PW & $+$1.42 & $+$4.17 \\
\addlinespace
Qwen-14B $\times$ DeBERTa-NLI & PW & $-$0.21 & $+$0.33 \\
Mistral $\times$ DeBERTa-NLI & FOLIO & $-$0.47 & $+$2.94 \\
\addlinespace
Mistral $\times$ SICA & FOLIO & $-$2.93 & $+$3.43 \\
Qwen-14B $\times$ SICA & PW & $-$4.21 & $-$2.50 \\
Mistral $\times$ SICA & PW & $-$4.61 & $-$2.83 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Supporting Diagnostics}
\label{sec:diagnostics}

Further diagnostics confirm the mechanism (Appendices~\ref{app:extraction}--\ref{app:entropy}). One positive signal emerges: Z3-based semantic deduplication removes ${\sim}$10\% additional constraints beyond syntactic dedup and yields a small but significant gain ($p = 0.013$), confirming that the formal channel adds value when redundancy is reduced. The bottleneck is not Z3 but the quality of input constraints: confirmation bias ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$) overwhelms the deduplication gain.


\subsection{Remediation Analysis}
\label{sec:remediation}
\label{sec:interventions}

\looseness=-1 Twenty-seven remediation strategies spanning four categories---sampling diversity (temperature, multi-T, diverse prompting), extraction quality (few-shot, iterative refinement, confidence filtering), aggregation (constraint weighting, semantic dedup, MAX-SAT variants), and multi-agent (debate, self-refine, critique)---all produce neutral-to-negative results (Figure~\ref{fig:interventions-summary}; Tables~\ref{tab:method_interventions}, \ref{tab:remediation}, \ref{tab:additional_interventions}). Of these, 12 represent principled strategies targeting distinct failure modes (independent constraint generation, contrastive extraction, debate-augmented reasoning, etc.), while 15 are parameter variations (temperature, prompt template, few-shot count). At the method level, Debate achieves the lowest $\kappa = 0.15$ but collapses accuracy by $-18.6$\,pp ($p < 0.001$); all other methods cluster at $\kappa \approx 0.40$--$0.47$, confirming a same-model ceiling (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:kappa_generalization}). We note an important gap: we do not evaluate trained constraint extractors (e.g., fine-tuned on oracle annotations). Our oracle controls show the extraction-quality ceiling is $+29.90$\,pp---we consider trained extractors the most promising future direction.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{figures/fig_app_interventions_summary.pdf}
\caption{Summary of 27 intervention strategies grouped by four categories. All produce neutral-to-negative accuracy changes relative to Self-Consistency. The grey band indicates $\pm 1$\,pp noise zone.}
\label{fig:interventions-summary}
\end{figure*}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Self-Consistency and extensions.}
SC selects the most frequent answer across chain-of-thought traces \citep{wang2023sc}. Extensions include universal SC for free-form generation \citep{chen2024usc}, confidence weighting \citep{taubenfeld2025cisc}, diverse prompting with step-aware verification \citep{li2023diverse}, program-based vote filtering \citep{toh2024notallvotes}, and reasoning-aware filtering \citep{wan2024rasc, kim2026reasc}. \citet{loo2025reevaluating} show that SC gains plateau early; \citet{byerly2026selfconsistency} further analyze SC's limitations in mathematical reasoning. Alternative aggregation strategies beyond majority voting \citep{ai2025beyondmajority} have been proposed but do not address the underlying correlation problem. Our Condorcet analysis explains this: $\kappa = 0.21$--$0.82$ compresses $K_{\text{eff}}$ to 1.20--3.62 for $K = 12$ (\S\ref{sec:effective-k}).

\paragraph{Neuro-symbolic reasoning.}
We distinguish three paradigms. \emph{Specification-generation} (Logic-LM \citep{pan2023logiclm}, LINC \citep{olausson2023linc}, SatLM \citep{ye2024satlm}) translates problem descriptions into formal specifications before any answer is generated; LINC documents ${\sim}$40\% translation errors even at this level. \emph{Program-generation} \citep{gao2023pal,chen2023program,lyu2023faithful} produces executable code from problem statements. Both paradigms access the problem before generating candidate answers. \emph{Trace-extraction} (this work) operates post-hoc on reasoning traces that already contain candidates, inheriting confirmation bias (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). SatLM provides a natural control: it succeeds because problem-level formalization precedes answer generation, whereas trace-level extraction cannot avoid contamination (\S\ref{sec:mirage}). Recent extensions \citep{ryu2025clover, xu2026dylos, li2025aristotle, wang2025fover, singh2026verge, luo2025logicagent, miya2025eidoku} introduce capabilities beyond the reasoning model. \citet{amonkar2025realitycheck} show formalization degrades with complexity; \citet{chan2026position} argue logical soundness is unreliable for neurosymbolic verification. Our oracle controls confirm the mechanism is sound ($+29.90$/$+14.22$\,pp FOLIO, $+17.17$\,pp ProofWriter); the failure localizes to self-extraction (\S\ref{sec:oracle}).

\paragraph{Test-time compute and verification.}
Scaling test-time computation through additional traces \citep{snell2025scaling} or structured search \citep{yao2023tree} faces the same correlation challenge: same-model attempts collapse into correlated solutions. Reasoning models (o1, DeepSeek-R1 \citep{guo2025deepseekr1}, QwQ \citep{qwen2024qwq}) extend thinking time but remain subject to correlated same-model voting: QwQ-32B produces $\kappa = 0.91$ and $C_0 = 2.6\%$, suggesting that longer reasoning chains intensify homogenization rather than alleviating it. Trained verifiers offer an alternative \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k, lightman2024prm, wang2024mathshepherd}; multi-agent debate refines answers through discussion \citep{liang2023encouraging, du2023improving}. Self-verification \citep{weng2023selfverification}, self-refinement \citep{madaan2023selfrefine}, tool-augmented critique \citep{gou2024critic}, and formal CoT verification \citep{zhu2025vericot} report gains, but none systematically tests self-extracted constraints under formal solvers. We find the bottleneck shifts from the verifier to the extractor: self-extracted constraints inherit the model's bias regardless of verification formalism (\S\ref{sec:confirmation-bias}). Self-correction without external feedback degrades performance \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification}; our diagnostics (BR, Eff-$K$, process-$\kappa$) predict when trace-aggregation will fail.

\paragraph{CJT and positioning.}
CJT \citep{condorcet1785} guarantees convergence under independent voting; \citet{ladha1992correlated} show correlated voters reduce effective ensemble size; \citet{lefort2024condorcet_llm} apply CJT to LLM classification. \citet{kim2025correlated} demonstrate correlated errors in LLM outputs. Concurrent work by \citet{kuai2026independent} proposes BEI and CIG to audit cross-model behavioral dependencies; our process-$\kappa$ and $K_\text{eff}$ are complementary, operating at the reasoning-process level within same-model traces and identifying a specific causal mechanism (SCB) that behavioral metrics miss.
Prior self-correction studies \citep{huang2024selfcorrect, stechly2025selfverification} establish qualitative negative results; we contribute quantitative diagnostics ($K_\text{eff}$, BR, $C_D$). SCB is distinct from CoT unfaithfulness \citep{turpin2024language} and CoT-level confirmation bias \citep{wan2025unveiling, jhaveri2026failing}: the latter concern whether stated reasoning reflects actual computation, while SCB concerns whether \emph{self-extracted formal constraints} mirror the model's answer distribution. Formal Z3-checked verification inherits the same bias ($\text{BR} = 4.97\times$)---invisible to prior work on natural-language self-correction.

